<%# A data-only node appended to the hidden goal feed on the live page. The
    page's MutationObserver reads these data-* attrs, fires a toast AND the
    scoring animation on the game tile, then removes the node. NO <script> here —
    Turbo doesn't execute scripts inside broadcast templates, which is why every
    value the page needs is an attribute.

    Locals: event ("goal"|"final"), goal (Goal|nil), team (Team|nil),
    player (Player|nil), game (Game).

    The slug/type/colour attrs were added when the contest page adopted the
    league board's tile: a toast only needs to name the team, but lighting the
    right ROW in the right COLOURS needs to identify it. Deliberately the same
    attribute names live/_event_feed_item uses, so the two feeds stay readable
    by the same eyes. %>
<div data-event="<%= event %>"
     data-game-slug="<%= game.slug %>"
     data-team-slug="<%= team&.slug %>"
     data-team="<%= team ? (team.short_name.presence || team.name) : "" %>"
     data-team-emoji="<%= team&.emoji %>"
     data-team-name="<%= team&.name %>"
     <%# The banner's own field and ink. Same two attributes live/_event_feed_item
         carries, because the two feeds raise the same banner. %>
     data-team-bg="<%= team&.card_background %>"
     data-team-fg="<%= team&.card_mascot %>"
     data-player="<%= player&.name %>"
     <%# The RAW type, not the label — the page keys its per-type animations off
         this, and "2-Point Conversion" is a display string that will change. %>
     data-scoring-type="<%= goal&.scoring_type %>"
     data-label="<%= goal&.scoring_label %>"
     data-points="<%= goal&.points %>"
     <%# The two wedge colours for the card's glow ring: the mascot accent, and
         the team's extra brand colour where it curates one (falling back to the
         light), so a two-colour team lights two wedges and a one-colour team
         reads as a single hue rather than a mismatch. %>
     <%# team_glow_pair, not palette[:glow]: the ring says WHOSE score this was,
         and a team with no curated alt colour returned its accent twice and drew
         a flat one-colour ring. See TeamColorsHelper#team_glow_pair. %>
     <% glow_a, glow_b = team ? team_glow_pair(team) : [nil, nil] %>
     data-glow-a="<%= glow_a %>"
     data-glow-b="<%= glow_b %>"
     <%# THE SCORER, for the focus panel's reveal. `scorer` is what ESPN's play
         text named; `scorer-headshot` is a cached S3 URL and is legitimately
         blank when we hold no athlete for them, which the card renders as
         initials. `reveals` is the model's own verdict on whether this score
         earns the takeover (touchdowns and field goals) — decided in Ruby so
         the rule lives in one place rather than being re-derived in JS. %>
     data-scorer="<%= goal&.scorer_name %>"
     data-scorer-slug="<%= goal&.scorer_slug %>"
     data-scorer-headshot="<%= goal&.scorer_headshot_url(width: 400) %>"
     data-reveals="<%= goal&.reveals_scorer? ? "1" : "" %>"
     data-play-description="<%= goal&.play_description %>"
     <%# The card names the team the way the TILE does — location above, mascot
         below in the team's own accent — so the two read as one object. Same
         palette the tile uses, passed through rather than recomputed in JS. %>
     data-team-location="<%= team&.location %>"
     data-team-mascot="<%= team ? (team.mascot.presence || team.short_name || team.name) : "" %>"
     data-accent="<%= team ? team_card_palette(team)[:accent] : "" %>"
     data-location-color="<%= team ? team_card_palette(team)[:location] : "" %>"
     data-home-team="<%= game.home_team&.short_name || game.home_team_slug %>"
     data-away-team="<%= game.away_team&.short_name || game.away_team_slug %>"
     data-home-score="<%= game.home_score.to_i %>"
     data-away-score="<%= game.away_score.to_i %>"></div>
